/* Egyptian book — Pediatrics; Accepted sources only. */
window.__SHB_EGYPTIAN_BOOK_QUESTIONS__=(window.__SHB_EGYPTIAN_BOOK_QUESTIONS__||[]).concat([{"id":"EB-paediatrics-nutrition-accepted-Q1","spec":"eb-nutrition","topic":0,"tag":"Egyptian book","kind":"Other","q":"Malnutrition associated with calorie and protein deficiency","o":["Protein energy malnutrition(PEM)","Marasmus","Both of the above","None of the above"],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":0,"ex":"Protein-energy malnutrition (PEM) is undernutrition involving inadequate energy and protein intake. Marasmus and kwashiorkor are clinical forms of severe acute malnutrition; therefore the broad category includes both.","more":"Protein-energy malnutrition (PEM) is undernutrition involving inadequate energy and protein intake. Marasmus and kwashiorkor are clinical forms of severe acute malnutrition; therefore the broad category includes both.\n\nCurriculum alignment: Growth & Nutrition 2024, pp.10–13 (malnutrition and PEM classification).\n\nHigh-yield point: Marasmus is classically severe wasting without edema; kwashiorkor has nutritional edema.","ao":true,"eb":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q1 — Protein-energy malnutrition","aoSource":"MCQ د عرفة متقسم · PDF sequence page 19 · printed page 28 · answer-key page 34 · original question 1.","aoSystem":"التغذية","aoPart":"","kx":false,"ebSourceFile":"Paediatrics_Nutrition_Accepted.md","_genhy":false},{"id":"EB-paediatrics-nutrition-accepted-Q5","spec":"eb-nutrition","topic":0,"tag":"Egyptian book","kind":"Investigation","q":"The parameter s used for nutritional assessment include, except","o":["Clinical","Anthropometric","Biochemical","Non of the above"],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":3,"ex":"Nutritional assessment combines clinical examination, anthropometry, laboratory/biochemical assessment and dietary history. Since all three listed methods are used, “none of the above” is the exception.","more":"Nutritional assessment combines clinical examination, anthropometry, laboratory/biochemical assessment and dietary history. Since all three listed methods are used, “none of the above” is the exception.\n\nCurriculum alignment: Growth & Nutrition 2024, pp.6–8 (MUAC, skinfold, laboratory and food-intake assessment).\n\nHigh-yield point: Assessment is multidimensional; no single measure is sufficient.","ao":true,"eb":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q5 — Components of nutritional assessment","aoSource":"MCQ د عرفة متقسم · PDF sequence page 19 · printed page 28 · answer-key page 34 · original question 5.","aoSystem":"التغذية","aoPart":"","kx":false,"ebSourceFile":"Paediatrics_Nutrition_Accepted.md","_genhy":false},{"id":"EB-paediatrics-nutrition-accepted-Q9","spec":"eb-nutrition","topic":0,"tag":"Egyptian book","kind":"Investigation","q":"In shakir tape, themalnutritionisdetectedby","o":["MAC less than12.5 cms","MAC less than12 cms","MAC less than11 cms","MAC less than11.5 cms"],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":0,"ex":"The supplied course notes state that a normal MUAC in children aged 1–4 years is above 12.5 cm, with 11.5–12.5 cm described as borderline/subclinical and below 11.5 cm as severe malnutrition. The source item’s <12.5 cm threshold includes both borderline and severe categories, so it is a broad screening threshold rather than the severe-malnutrition cut-off.","more":"The supplied course notes state that a normal MUAC in children aged 1–4 years is above 12.5 cm, with 11.5–12.5 cm described as borderline/subclinical and below 11.5 cm as severe malnutrition. The source item’s <12.5 cm threshold includes both borderline and severe categories, so it is a broad screening threshold rather than the severe-malnutrition cut-off.\n\nCurriculum alignment: Growth & Nutrition 2024, p.6 (MUAC and Shakir tape thresholds).\n\nHigh-yield point: MUAC cut-offs depend on age and the guideline being used; do not apply a single threshold to infants under six months.","ao":true,"eb":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q9 — Shakir tape threshold for malnutrition","aoSource":"MCQ د عرفة متقسم · PDF sequence page 19 · printed page 28 · answer-key page 34 · original question 9.","aoSystem":"التغذية","aoPart":"","kx":false,"ebSourceFile":"Paediatrics_Nutrition_Accepted.md","_genhy":false},{"id":"EB-paediatrics-nutrition-accepted-Q11","spec":"eb-nutrition","topic":0,"tag":"Egyptian book","kind":"Other","q":"The clinical parameter most commonly affected in PEM","o":["Height","Weight","Mid arm circumference(MAC)","Head circumference"],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":1,"ex":"Weight is commonly affected early in protein-energy malnutrition and is routinely followed in growth monitoring. MUAC is a useful screening indicator and may be the earliest field indicator in some settings; the wording “most commonly affected” should be distinguished from “best indicator”.","more":"Weight is commonly affected early in protein-energy malnutrition and is routinely followed in growth monitoring. MUAC is a useful screening indicator and may be the earliest field indicator in some settings; the wording “most commonly affected” should be distinguished from “best indicator”.\n\nCurriculum alignment: Growth & Nutrition 2024, pp.4, 6–7, 10–13 (weight, MUAC, wasting and PEM).\n\nHigh-yield point: Weight-for-height identifies wasting; height-for-age identifies stunting.","ao":true,"eb":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q11 — Most commonly affected parameter in PEM","aoSource":"MCQ د عرفة متقسم · PDF sequence page 19 · printed page 28 · answer-key page 34 · original question 11.","aoSystem":"التغذية","aoPart":"","kx":false,"ebSourceFile":"Paediatrics_Nutrition_Accepted.md","_genhy":false},{"id":"EB-paediatrics-nutrition-accepted-Q14","spec":"eb-nutrition","topic":0,"tag":"Egyptian book","kind":"Other","q":"The calorie requirement of the one year old child","o":["90 cal/kg/d","120 cal/kg/d","110 cal/kg/d","l00 cal/kg/d"],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":-1,"ex":"The course table gives approximately 95 kcal/kg/day for ages 1–3 years. Among the options, 90 kcal/kg/day is the closest conventional value, but the source answer key is absent. This is an educational interpretation, not a recovered source answer.","more":"The course table gives approximately 95 kcal/kg/day for ages 1–3 years. Among the options, 90 kcal/kg/day is the closest conventional value, but the source answer key is absent. This is an educational interpretation, not a recovered source answer.\n\nCurriculum alignment: Growth & Nutrition 2024, p.9 (energy requirements: 1–3 years approximately 95 kcal/kg/24 h).\n\nHigh-yield point: Energy needs are age-specific; the infant requirement is higher per kilogram than that of older children.","ao":true,"eb":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":true,"aoTitle":"Q14 — Daily calorie requirement of a one-year-old","aoSource":"MCQ د عرفة متقسم · PDF sequence page 20 · printed page 29 · answer-key page غير موجود · original question 14.","aoSystem":"التغذية","aoPart":"","kx":true,"ebSourceFile":"Paediatrics_Nutrition_Accepted.md","_genhy":false},{"id":"EB-paediatrics-nutrition-accepted-Q15","spec":"eb-nutrition","topic":0,"tag":"Egyptian book","kind":"Pathophysiology","q":"The calorie requirement is more in infants because of","o":["Higher growth rate and B MR","Higher growth rate and decreased B MR","Decreased growth rate and B MR","Decreased growth rate and increased B MR"],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":0,"ex":"Infants have high energy needs per kilogram because of rapid growth and a relatively high basal metabolic rate.","more":"Infants have high energy needs per kilogram because of rapid growth and a relatively high basal metabolic rate.\n\nCurriculum alignment: Growth & Nutrition 2024, p.9 (infant vulnerability to poor nutrition and energy requirements).\n\nHigh-yield point: Rapid growth plus limited nutritional reserves makes infants vulnerable to reduced intake during illness.","ao":true,"eb":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q15 — Why infants need more calories per kilogram","aoSource":"MCQ د عرفة متقسم · PDF sequence page 20 · printed page 29 · answer-key page 34 · original question 15.","aoSystem":"التغذية","aoPart":"","kx":false,"ebSourceFile":"Paediatrics_Nutrition_Accepted.md","_genhy":false},{"id":"EB-paediatrics-nutrition-accepted-Q18","spec":"eb-nutrition","topic":0,"tag":"Egyptian book","kind":"Pathophysiology","q":"Essential amino acids","o":["Cannot be synthesized in body","Can be synthesized in body","Shouldnottobesupplement","Have to be substituted"],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":0,"ex":"Essential amino acids cannot be synthesized by the human body in adequate amounts and therefore must be supplied in the diet.","more":"Essential amino acids cannot be synthesized by the human body in adequate amounts and therefore must be supplied in the diet.\n\nCurriculum alignment: Growth & Nutrition 2024, p.9 (protein requirements and balanced diet).\n\nHigh-yield point: Essential amino acids differ by age and metabolic state; some are conditionally essential in infancy or prematurity.","ao":true,"eb":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q18 — Definition of essential amino acids","aoSource":"MCQ د عرفة متقسم · PDF sequence page 20 · printed page 29 · answer-key page 34 · original question 18.","aoSystem":"التغذية","aoPart":"","kx":false,"ebSourceFile":"Paediatrics_Nutrition_Accepted.md","_genhy":false},{"id":"EB-paediatrics-nutrition-accepted-Q19","spec":"eb-nutrition","topic":0,"tag":"Egyptian book","kind":"Other","q":"The amino acid essential during infancy only","o":["Taurine","Leucine","Isoleucine","Histidine"],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":-1,"ex":"Taurine is often considered conditionally essential in early infancy, particularly in preterm infants, because endogenous synthesis and bile-acid conjugation capacity may be limited. Leucine, isoleucine and histidine are essential amino acids throughout life rather than only in infancy. The source answer is absent, so no source key can be asserted.","more":"Taurine is often considered conditionally essential in early infancy, particularly in preterm infants, because endogenous synthesis and bile-acid conjugation capacity may be limited. Leucine, isoleucine and histidine are essential amino acids throughout life rather than only in infancy. The source answer is absent, so no source key can be asserted.\n\nCurriculum alignment: Growth & Nutrition 2024, p.9 (infant nutritional requirements; no explicit taurine table located).\n\nHigh-yield point: “Conditionally essential” is more precise than saying taurine is universally essential only in infancy.","ao":true,"eb":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":true,"aoTitle":"Q19 — Amino acid essential during infancy","aoSource":"MCQ د عرفة متقسم · PDF sequence page 21 · printed page 30 · answer-key page غير موجود · original question 19.","aoSystem":"التغذية","aoPart":"","kx":true,"ebSourceFile":"Paediatrics_Nutrition_Accepted.md","_genhy":false},{"id":"EB-paediatrics-nutrition-accepted-Q24","spec":"eb-nutrition","topic":0,"tag":"Egyptian book","kind":"Other","q":"The protein requirement for1 year old child is","o":["1 g/kg/d","2 g/kg/day","3 g/kg/d","4 g/kg/d"],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":-1,"ex":"The supplied course table gives about 1.8 g/kg/day for ages 1–3 years. Among the options, 2 g/kg/day is the nearest rounded value. The original extraction does not provide an answer-key entry; this is an educational interpretation only.","more":"The supplied course table gives about 1.8 g/kg/day for ages 1–3 years. Among the options, 2 g/kg/day is the nearest rounded value. The original extraction does not provide an answer-key entry; this is an educational interpretation only.\n\nCurriculum alignment: Growth & Nutrition 2024, p.9 (protein requirement 1–3 years: approximately 1.8 g/kg/24 h).\n\nHigh-yield point: Use the age-specific table in the course; do not generalize infant protein requirements to toddlers.","ao":true,"eb":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":true,"aoTitle":"Q24 — Protein requirement at one year","aoSource":"MCQ د عرفة متقسم · PDF sequence page 21 · printed page 30 · answer-key page غير موجود · original question 24.","aoSystem":"التغذية","aoPart":"","kx":true,"ebSourceFile":"Paediatrics_Nutrition_Accepted.md","_genhy":false},{"id":"EB-paediatrics-nutrition-accepted-Q27","spec":"eb-nutrition","topic":0,"tag":"Egyptian book","kind":"Diagnosis","q":"The clinical signs of kwashiorkor include the following, except","o":["Edema","Skin changes","Hair changes","Bone change"],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":3,"ex":"Kwashiorkor is characterized by nutritional edema, skin and hair changes, apathy/irritability, fatty liver and growth failure. Primary bone changes are not a defining clinical sign in the classic description.","more":"Kwashiorkor is characterized by nutritional edema, skin and hair changes, apathy/irritability, fatty liver and growth failure. Primary bone changes are not a defining clinical sign in the classic description.\n\nCurriculum alignment: Growth & Nutrition 2024, pp.12–14 (kwashiorkor features, skin/hair changes, edema and complications).\n\nHigh-yield point: Edema can mask severe wasting; assess nutritional status even when body weight appears less reduced.","ao":true,"eb":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q27 — Clinical signs of kwashiorkor: exception","aoSource":"MCQ د عرفة متقسم · PDF sequence page 21 · printed page 30 · answer-key page 34 · original question 27.","aoSystem":"التغذية","aoPart":"","kx":false,"ebSourceFile":"Paediatrics_Nutrition_Accepted.md","_genhy":false},{"id":"EB-paediatrics-nutrition-accepted-Q38","spec":"eb-nutrition","topic":0,"tag":"Egyptian book","kind":"Other","q":"Essential fatty acids include the following, except","o":["Linoleic acid","Linolinicacid","Arachidonic acid","Palmitic acid"],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":3,"ex":"Linoleic acid (omega-6) and alpha-linolenic acid (omega-3) are essential fatty acids. Arachidonic acid can be conditionally essential, especially in infancy; palmitic acid is a saturated fatty acid and is not essential.","more":"Linoleic acid (omega-6) and alpha-linolenic acid (omega-3) are essential fatty acids. Arachidonic acid can be conditionally essential, especially in infancy; palmitic acid is a saturated fatty acid and is not essential.\n\nCurriculum alignment: Growth & Nutrition 2024, p.9 (balanced diet and lipid requirements); breast-milk lipid discussion, p.32.\n\nHigh-yield point: Essential fatty acids support membrane structure, growth and neurodevelopment.","ao":true,"eb":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q38 — Essential fatty acids: exception","aoSource":"MCQ د عرفة متقسم · PDF sequence page 22 · printed page 31 · answer-key page 34 · original question 38.","aoSystem":"التغذية","aoPart":"","kx":false,"ebSourceFile":"Paediatrics_Nutrition_Accepted.md","_genhy":false},{"id":"EB-paediatrics-nutrition-accepted-Q45","spec":"eb-nutrition","topic":0,"tag":"Egyptian book","kind":"Other","q":"Vitamin A is rich in following vegetable","o":["Carrot","Green leafy vegetable","All of the above,","None of the above"],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":2,"ex":"Carrots provide beta-carotene, and green leafy vegetables are also rich sources of provitamin A carotenoids. Thus both listed vegetable groups contribute vitamin A precursors.","more":"Carrots provide beta-carotene, and green leafy vegetables are also rich sources of provitamin A carotenoids. Thus both listed vegetable groups contribute vitamin A precursors.\n\nCurriculum alignment: Growth & Nutrition 2024, p.14 (vitamin A deficiency and food-related nutritional content); p.9 (micronutrients).\n\nHigh-yield point: Dietary beta-carotene is converted to retinol according to the body’s needs and bioavailability.","ao":true,"eb":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q45 — Vegetable sources rich in vitamin A","aoSource":"MCQ د عرفة متقسم · PDF sequence page 22 · printed page 31 · answer-key page 34 · original question 45.","aoSystem":"التغذية","aoPart":"","kx":false,"ebSourceFile":"Paediatrics_Nutrition_Accepted.md","_genhy":false},{"id":"EB-paediatrics-nutrition-accepted-Q46","spec":"eb-nutrition","topic":0,"tag":"Egyptian book","kind":"Other","q":"The dietary source of vitamin A includes all, except","o":["Whole milk","Liver","Carrot","Maize"],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":-1,"ex":"Liver and whole milk can provide preformed vitamin A, while carrots provide provitamin A carotenoids. Maize is not generally a rich source of vitamin A unless it is a biofortified yellow/orange variety. The source answer is missing.","more":"Liver and whole milk can provide preformed vitamin A, while carrots provide provitamin A carotenoids. Maize is not generally a rich source of vitamin A unless it is a biofortified yellow/orange variety. The source answer is missing.\n\nCurriculum alignment: Growth & Nutrition 2024, p.9 (vitamin requirements) and p.14 (vitamin A deficiency); food-source detail is an educational extension.\n\nHigh-yield point: Preformed vitamin A is found in animal foods; carotenoids are found in colored vegetables.","ao":true,"eb":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":true,"aoTitle":"Q46 — Dietary sources of vitamin A: exception","aoSource":"MCQ د عرفة متقسم · PDF sequence page 22 · printed page 31 · answer-key page غير موجود · original question 46.","aoSystem":"التغذية","aoPart":"","kx":true,"ebSourceFile":"Paediatrics_Nutrition_Accepted.md","_genhy":false},{"id":"EB-paediatrics-nutrition-accepted-Q47","spec":"eb-nutrition","topic":0,"tag":"Egyptian book","kind":"Diagnosis","q":"The early clinical feature of vitamin A deficiency","o":["Bitotspot","Xerophthalmia","Night blindness","Phrenoderma"],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":2,"ex":"Night blindness is an early functional manifestation of vitamin A deficiency because retinal function is impaired before more advanced ocular surface disease develops.","more":"Night blindness is an early functional manifestation of vitamin A deficiency because retinal function is impaired before more advanced ocular surface disease develops.\n\nCurriculum alignment: Growth & Nutrition 2024, p.14 (vitamin A deficiency: xerophthalmia, Bitot spots and blindness).\n\nHigh-yield point: Night blindness may precede conjunctival xerosis, Bitot spots and corneal disease.","ao":true,"eb":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q47 — Early clinical feature of vitamin A deficiency","aoSource":"MCQ د عرفة متقسم · PDF sequence page 23 · printed page 32 · answer-key page 34 · original question 47.","aoSystem":"التغذية","aoPart":"","kx":false,"ebSourceFile":"Paediatrics_Nutrition_Accepted.md","_genhy":false},{"id":"EB-paediatrics-nutrition-accepted-Q48","spec":"eb-nutrition","topic":0,"tag":"Egyptian book","kind":"Diagnosis","q":"The clinical sign in eye in vitamin A deficiency, except","o":["Xerosis","Bitotspot","Retinal detachment","Xerophthalmia"],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":2,"ex":"Vitamin A deficiency causes night blindness and xerophthalmia, including conjunctival/corneal xerosis and Bitot spots; retinal detachment is not a typical direct manifestation.","more":"Vitamin A deficiency causes night blindness and xerophthalmia, including conjunctival/corneal xerosis and Bitot spots; retinal detachment is not a typical direct manifestation.\n\nCurriculum alignment: Growth & Nutrition 2024, p.14 (vitamin A deficiency).\n\nHigh-yield point: Severe deficiency can progress to keratomalacia and irreversible blindness.","ao":true,"eb":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q48 — Ocular sign not caused by vitamin A deficiency","aoSource":"MCQ د عرفة متقسم · PDF sequence page 23 · printed page 32 · answer-key page 34 · original question 48.","aoSystem":"التغذية","aoPart":"","kx":false,"ebSourceFile":"Paediatrics_Nutrition_Accepted.md","_genhy":false},{"id":"EB-paediatrics-nutrition-accepted-Q53","spec":"eb-nutrition","topic":0,"tag":"Egyptian book","kind":"Pathophysiology","q":"The deficiency of B complex produces acute cardiac failure","o":["Biotin","Cyanocobalamine","Thiamine","Riboflavin"],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":2,"ex":"Thiamine (vitamin B1) deficiency causes beriberi; wet beriberi can cause high-output cardiac failure and edema.","more":"Thiamine (vitamin B1) deficiency causes beriberi; wet beriberi can cause high-output cardiac failure and edema.\n\nCurriculum alignment: Growth & Nutrition 2024, p.14 (vitamin deficiency complications); p.9 (micronutrients).\n\nHigh-yield point: Consider thiamine deficiency in malnourished infants with cardiac failure, lactic acidosis or neurologic signs.","ao":true,"eb":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q53 — B-complex deficiency causing acute cardiac failure","aoSource":"MCQ د عرفة متقسم · PDF sequence page 23 · printed page 32 · answer-key page 34 · original question 53.","aoSystem":"التغذية","aoPart":"","kx":false,"ebSourceFile":"Paediatrics_Nutrition_Accepted.md","_genhy":false},{"id":"EB-paediatrics-nutrition-accepted-Q56","spec":"eb-nutrition","topic":0,"tag":"Egyptian book","kind":"Pathophysiology","q":"Pyridoxine deficiency lead s to","o":["Convulsions","Anaemia","Both of the above","None of the above"],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":2,"ex":"Vitamin B6 (pyridoxine) deficiency can cause seizures, particularly in infants, and sideroblastic/microcytic anemia due to impaired heme synthesis. Both listed manifestations are recognized.","more":"Vitamin B6 (pyridoxine) deficiency can cause seizures, particularly in infants, and sideroblastic/microcytic anemia due to impaired heme synthesis. Both listed manifestations are recognized.\n\nCurriculum alignment: Growth & Nutrition 2024, p.14 (vitamin deficiency effects); infant nutritional requirements.\n\nHigh-yield point: Consider B6 deficiency in unexplained infantile seizures or refractory anemia, especially with relevant drug exposure or metabolic disease.","ao":true,"eb":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q56 — Manifestations of pyridoxine deficiency","aoSource":"MCQ د عرفة متقسم · PDF sequence page 24 · printed page 33 · answer-key page 34 · original question 56.","aoSystem":"التغذية","aoPart":"","kx":false,"ebSourceFile":"Paediatrics_Nutrition_Accepted.md","_genhy":false},{"id":"EB-paediatrics-nutrition-accepted-Q57","spec":"eb-nutrition","topic":0,"tag":"Egyptian book","kind":"Pathophysiology","q":"The predominant symptoms of the folic acid deficiency","o":["Constipation","Diarrhoea","Anaemia","Skin changes"],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":2,"ex":"Folate deficiency impairs DNA synthesis and causes megaloblastic anemia; gastrointestinal and mucosal symptoms may occur, but anemia is the principal systemic manifestation.","more":"Folate deficiency impairs DNA synthesis and causes megaloblastic anemia; gastrointestinal and mucosal symptoms may occur, but anemia is the principal systemic manifestation.\n\nCurriculum alignment: Growth & Nutrition 2024, p.9 and p.14 (micronutrients and deficiency manifestations).\n\nHigh-yield point: Folate deficiency causes macrocytosis/megaloblastosis; distinguish from B12 deficiency because B12 can also cause neurologic disease.","ao":true,"eb":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q57 — Predominant manifestation of folic acid deficiency","aoSource":"MCQ د عرفة متقسم · PDF sequence page 24 · printed page 33 · answer-key page 34 · original question 57.","aoSystem":"التغذية","aoPart":"","kx":false,"ebSourceFile":"Paediatrics_Nutrition_Accepted.md","_genhy":false},{"id":"EB-paediatrics-nutrition-accepted-Q58","spec":"eb-nutrition","topic":0,"tag":"Egyptian book","kind":"Physiology","q":"Vitamin B12 is absorbed in","o":["Lowerpartofcaecum","Lower part of ileum","Upper part of ileum","Upperpartofcaecum"],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":1,"ex":"Vitamin B12 binds intrinsic factor in the stomach and the complex is absorbed in the terminal ileum.","more":"Vitamin B12 binds intrinsic factor in the stomach and the complex is absorbed in the terminal ileum.\n\nCurriculum alignment: GIT 2024–2025 and GIT 2025, absorption sections; Growth & Nutrition 2024, p.9 (vitamin requirements).\n\nHigh-yield point: Terminal ileal disease or resection can cause B12 deficiency.","ao":true,"eb":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q58 — Site of vitamin B12 absorption","aoSource":"MCQ د عرفة متقسم · PDF sequence page 24 · printed page 33 · answer-key page 34 · original question 58.","aoSystem":"التغذية","aoPart":"","kx":false,"ebSourceFile":"Paediatrics_Nutrition_Accepted.md","_genhy":false},{"id":"EB-paediatrics-nutrition-accepted-Q63","spec":"eb-nutrition","topic":0,"tag":"Egyptian book","kind":"Diagnosis","q":"The common clinical signs of scurvy include, except","o":["Pseudoparalysis","Bleeding gum","Bony tenderness","Congestive cardiac failure"],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":3,"ex":"Vitamin C deficiency causes defective collagen synthesis, leading to bleeding gums, bone pain/tenderness, subperiosteal hemorrhage and pseudoparalysis in infants. Congestive heart failure is not a typical sign of scurvy.","more":"Vitamin C deficiency causes defective collagen synthesis, leading to bleeding gums, bone pain/tenderness, subperiosteal hemorrhage and pseudoparalysis in infants. Congestive heart failure is not a typical sign of scurvy.\n\nCurriculum alignment: Growth & Nutrition 2024, p.14 (vitamin C deficiency/scurvy).\n\nHigh-yield point: Infantile scurvy can mimic infection or malignancy because of bone pain and irritability.","ao":true,"eb":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q63 — Clinical sign not typical of scurvy","aoSource":"MCQ د عرفة متقسم · PDF sequence page 24 · printed page 33 · answer-key page 34 · original question 63.","aoSystem":"التغذية","aoPart":"","kx":false,"ebSourceFile":"Paediatrics_Nutrition_Accepted.md","_genhy":false},{"id":"EB-paediatrics-nutrition-accepted-Q69","spec":"eb-nutrition","topic":0,"tag":"Egyptian book","kind":"Physiology","q":"Thechemicalcompound in the skin from which vitamin D3 isformed","o":["17-hydrocholesterol","7-dehydrocholesterol","17-dehydrocholesterol","7-hydrocholesterol"],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":1,"ex":"Ultraviolet B radiation converts 7-dehydrocholesterol in the skin to previtamin D3, which isomerizes to cholecalciferol (vitamin D3).","more":"Ultraviolet B radiation converts 7-dehydrocholesterol in the skin to previtamin D3, which isomerizes to cholecalciferol (vitamin D3).\n\nCurriculum alignment: Growth & Nutrition 2024, p.19 (vitamin D metabolism).\n\nHigh-yield point: Vitamin D is hydroxylated in liver to 25-OH-D and in kidney to active 1,25-(OH)2-D.","ao":true,"eb":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q69 — Skin precursor of vitamin D3","aoSource":"MCQ د عرفة متقسم · PDF sequence page 25 · printed page 34 · answer-key page 34 · original question 69.","aoSystem":"التغذية","aoPart":"","kx":false,"ebSourceFile":"Paediatrics_Nutrition_Accepted.md","_genhy":false},{"id":"EB-paediatrics-nutrition-accepted-Q70","spec":"eb-nutrition","topic":0,"tag":"Egyptian book","kind":"Physiology","q":"The metabolic active form of vitamin D","o":["1-24 DHCC","1-22 DHCC","1-23 DHCC","1-25 DHCC"],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":3,"ex":"The active hormonal form of vitamin D is 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D (calcitriol), produced mainly in the kidney by 1-alpha hydroxylation of 25-OH-D.","more":"The active hormonal form of vitamin D is 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D (calcitriol), produced mainly in the kidney by 1-alpha hydroxylation of 25-OH-D.\n\nCurriculum alignment: Growth & Nutrition 2024, p.19 (liver/kidney vitamin D metabolism diagram).\n\nHigh-yield point: 25-OH-D is the main circulating storage/status marker; calcitriol is the active hormone.","ao":true,"eb":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q70 — Metabolically active form of vitamin D","aoSource":"MCQ د عرفة متقسم · PDF sequence page 25 · printed page 34 · answer-key page 34 · original question 70.","aoSystem":"التغذية","aoPart":"","kx":false,"ebSourceFile":"Paediatrics_Nutrition_Accepted.md","_genhy":false},{"id":"EB-paediatrics-nutrition-accepted-Q72","spec":"eb-nutrition","topic":0,"tag":"Egyptian book","kind":"Diagnosis","q":"The clinical signs of rickets include the following, except","o":["Frontal bossing","Beadingoftheribs","Ricketyrosary","Scorbaticrosary"],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":3,"ex":"Rickets causes frontal bossing, rachitic rosary (costochondral beading), widened wrists/ankles, craniotabes and delayed closure of fontanelle. “Scorbutic rosary” belongs to scurvy, not rickets.","more":"Rickets causes frontal bossing, rachitic rosary (costochondral beading), widened wrists/ankles, craniotabes and delayed closure of fontanelle. “Scorbutic rosary” belongs to scurvy, not rickets.\n\nCurriculum alignment: Growth & Nutrition 2024, pp.19–21 (rickets definition, pathogenesis and clinical features).\n\nHigh-yield point: Rachitic rosary = costochondral enlargement; scorbutic rosary is a different descriptive term.","ao":true,"eb":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q72 — Clinical sign not seen in rickets","aoSource":"MCQ د عرفة متقسم · PDF sequence page 25 · printed page 34 · answer-key page 34 · original question 72.","aoSystem":"التغذية","aoPart":"","kx":false,"ebSourceFile":"Paediatrics_Nutrition_Accepted.md","_genhy":false},{"id":"EB-paediatrics-nutrition-accepted-Q74","spec":"eb-nutrition","topic":0,"tag":"Egyptian book","kind":"Investigation","q":"All are investigations required for the diagnosis of rickets except:","o":["Serum calcium","Phosphorous","Alkaline phosphate","Serum magnesium"],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":3,"ex":"The usual biochemical evaluation of nutritional rickets includes calcium, phosphate, alkaline phosphatase, 25-OH vitamin D and often PTH. Magnesium may be checked in selected patients, particularly with refractory hypocalcemia, but is not one of the core routine tests in the classic list.","more":"The usual biochemical evaluation of nutritional rickets includes calcium, phosphate, alkaline phosphatase, 25-OH vitamin D and often PTH. Magnesium may be checked in selected patients, particularly with refractory hypocalcemia, but is not one of the core routine tests in the classic list.\n\nCurriculum alignment: Growth & Nutrition 2024, pp.19–21 (rickets investigation and pathogenesis).\n\nHigh-yield point: Interpret calcium and phosphate alongside age-specific alkaline phosphatase and 25-OH vitamin D.","ao":true,"eb":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q74 — Investigation not routinely required for rickets diagnosis","aoSource":"MCQ د عرفة متقسم · PDF sequence page 25 · printed page 34 · answer-key page 34 · original question 74.","aoSystem":"التغذية","aoPart":"","kx":false,"ebSourceFile":"Paediatrics_Nutrition_Accepted.md","_genhy":false},{"id":"EB-paediatrics-nutrition-accepted-Q75","spec":"eb-nutrition","topic":0,"tag":"Egyptian book","kind":"Investigation","q":"The first biochemical test to become abnormal in rickets","o":["Alkaline phosphatase","Serumphosphorous","Both of the above","None of the above"],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":0,"ex":"Alkaline phosphatase is often elevated early in active nutritional rickets, reflecting increased osteoblastic activity. Phosphate and calcium can also become abnormal, depending on disease stage and calcium intake.","more":"Alkaline phosphatase is often elevated early in active nutritional rickets, reflecting increased osteoblastic activity. Phosphate and calcium can also become abnormal, depending on disease stage and calcium intake.\n\nCurriculum alignment: Growth & Nutrition 2024, pp.19–21 (rickets pathogenesis and biochemical findings).\n\nHigh-yield point: A normal calcium does not exclude rickets; ALP and 25-OH vitamin D are important.","ao":true,"eb":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q75 — First biochemical test to become abnormal in rickets","aoSource":"MCQ د عرفة متقسم · PDF sequence page 25 · printed page 34 · answer-key page 34 · original question 75.","aoSystem":"التغذية","aoPart":"","kx":false,"ebSourceFile":"Paediatrics_Nutrition_Accepted.md","_genhy":false},{"id":"EB-paediatrics-nutrition-accepted-Q78","spec":"eb-nutrition","topic":0,"tag":"Egyptian book","kind":"Diagnosis","q":"The complication of rickets include the following, except","o":["Tetany","Tetanus","Respiratory infection","Fractures"],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":1,"ex":"Untreated rickets can cause hypocalcemic tetany, respiratory complications from chest wall deformity, fractures and long-term skeletal deformities. Tetanus is an infectious neurologic disease and not a rickets complication.","more":"Untreated rickets can cause hypocalcemic tetany, respiratory complications from chest wall deformity, fractures and long-term skeletal deformities. Tetanus is an infectious neurologic disease and not a rickets complication.\n\nCurriculum alignment: Growth & Nutrition 2024, pp.19–21 (rickets); vitamin D deficiency effects.\n\nHigh-yield point: Tetany is due to hypocalcemia; tetanus is due to tetanospasmin.","ao":true,"eb":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q78 — Complication of rickets: exception","aoSource":"MCQ د عرفة متقسم · PDF sequence page 26 · printed page 35 · answer-key page 34 · original question 78.","aoSystem":"التغذية","aoPart":"","kx":false,"ebSourceFile":"Paediatrics_Nutrition_Accepted.md","_genhy":false},{"id":"EB-paediatrics-nutrition-accepted-Q79","spec":"eb-nutrition","topic":0,"tag":"Egyptian book","kind":"Diagnosis","q":"The permanent sequence of the rickets include the following, except","o":["Stunting of growth","Bony deformities","Contractedpelvis","As c it is"],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":3,"ex":"Untreated rickets can cause stunting, residual bony deformities and pelvic deformity. Ascites is not a typical permanent skeletal sequela.","more":"Untreated rickets can cause stunting, residual bony deformities and pelvic deformity. Ascites is not a typical permanent skeletal sequela.\n\nCurriculum alignment: Growth & Nutrition 2024, pp.19–21 (rickets and complications).\n\nHigh-yield point: Prevention and early treatment avoid permanent deformity.","ao":true,"eb":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q79 — Permanent sequela of rickets: exception","aoSource":"MCQ د عرفة متقسم · PDF sequence page 26 · printed page 35 · answer-key page 34 · original question 79.","aoSystem":"التغذية","aoPart":"","kx":false,"ebSourceFile":"Paediatrics_Nutrition_Accepted.md","_genhy":false},{"id":"EB-paediatrics-nutrition-accepted-Q80","spec":"eb-nutrition","topic":0,"tag":"Egyptian book","kind":"Diagnosis","q":"The symptoms of vitamin D toxicity","o":["Constipation","Polyuria","Polydipsia","All of the above"],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":3,"ex":"Vitamin D toxicity usually causes hypercalcemia, with gastrointestinal symptoms, constipation, polyuria, polydipsia, dehydration and sometimes nephrocalcinosis. Thus all listed symptoms can occur.","more":"Vitamin D toxicity usually causes hypercalcemia, with gastrointestinal symptoms, constipation, polyuria, polydipsia, dehydration and sometimes nephrocalcinosis. Thus all listed symptoms can occur.\n\nCurriculum alignment: Growth & Nutrition 2024, pp.19–20 (vitamin D metabolism); toxicity is an extension beyond the brief course outline.\n\nHigh-yield point: Vitamin D toxicity is mediated mainly through hypercalcemia, not direct vitamin D effects alone.","ao":true,"eb":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q80 — Symptoms of vitamin D toxicity","aoSource":"MCQ د عرفة متقسم · PDF sequence page 26 · printed page 35 · answer-key page 34 · original question 80.","aoSystem":"التغذية","aoPart":"","kx":false,"ebSourceFile":"Paediatrics_Nutrition_Accepted.md","_genhy":false},{"id":"EB-paediatrics-nutrition-accepted-Q81","spec":"eb-nutrition","topic":0,"tag":"Egyptian book","kind":"Treatment","q":"Treatmentofhypervitaminoses D","o":["Calciumsupplement","Antibiotic","Ara chi to l","Steroids"],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":3,"ex":"Management starts by stopping vitamin D and calcium sources and treating hypercalcemia with hydration and monitoring. Glucocorticoids may be used in selected severe vitamin D-mediated hypercalcemia; other therapies may be needed depending on severity. Steroids are not a universal sole treatment.","more":"Management starts by stopping vitamin D and calcium sources and treating hypercalcemia with hydration and monitoring. Glucocorticoids may be used in selected severe vitamin D-mediated hypercalcemia; other therapies may be needed depending on severity. Steroids are not a universal sole treatment.\n\nCurriculum alignment: Growth & Nutrition 2024, pp.19–20 (vitamin D); specific toxicity management is an extended clinical application.\n\nHigh-yield point: Treat the hypercalcemia and stop the source; management depends on severity and renal status.","ao":true,"eb":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q81 — Treatment of hypervitaminosis D","aoSource":"MCQ د عرفة متقسم · PDF sequence page 26 · printed page 35 · answer-key page 34 · original question 81.","aoSystem":"التغذية","aoPart":"","kx":false,"ebSourceFile":"Paediatrics_Nutrition_Accepted.md","_genhy":false},{"id":"EB-paediatrics-nutrition-accepted-Q82","spec":"eb-nutrition","topic":0,"tag":"Egyptian book","kind":"Diagnosis","q":"Vitamin E deficiency produces","o":["Hae mor rha gic disease","Anaemia","Haemolytic anaemia","Disseminatedintravascularcoagulopathy(DIC)"],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":2,"ex":"Vitamin E deficiency can cause oxidative hemolysis and hemolytic anemia, particularly in premature infants or patients with fat malabsorption.","more":"Vitamin E deficiency can cause oxidative hemolysis and hemolytic anemia, particularly in premature infants or patients with fat malabsorption.\n\nCurriculum alignment: Growth & Nutrition 2024, p.14 (vitamin deficiency effects).\n\nHigh-yield point: Prematurity and cholestasis/malabsorption increase risk of vitamin E deficiency.","ao":true,"eb":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q82 — Vitamin E deficiency manifestation","aoSource":"MCQ د عرفة متقسم · PDF sequence page 27 · printed page 36 · answer-key page 34 · original question 82.","aoSystem":"التغذية","aoPart":"","kx":false,"ebSourceFile":"Paediatrics_Nutrition_Accepted.md","_genhy":false},{"id":"EB-paediatrics-nutrition-accepted-Q83","spec":"eb-nutrition","topic":0,"tag":"Egyptian book","kind":"Prevention","q":"The dose of vitamin Kin newborn","o":["1 mg","2 mg","3 mg","5 mg"],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":0,"ex":"Routine intramuscular vitamin K prophylaxis at birth is commonly 1 mg for term infants in many standard protocols; smaller doses are used for very-low-birth-weight infants according to local guidance.","more":"Routine intramuscular vitamin K prophylaxis at birth is commonly 1 mg for term infants in many standard protocols; smaller doses are used for very-low-birth-weight infants according to local guidance.\n\nCurriculum alignment: Neonatology 2024–2025, neonatal preventive care; Growth & Nutrition 2024, p.14 (vitamin K deficiency and bleeding).\n\nHigh-yield point: Do not confuse prophylaxis at birth with treatment doses for established vitamin K deficiency bleeding.","ao":true,"eb":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q83 — Vitamin K prophylaxis dose in the newborn","aoSource":"MCQ د عرفة متقسم · PDF sequence page 27 · printed page 36 · answer-key page 34 · original question 83.","aoSystem":"التغذية","aoPart":"","kx":false,"ebSourceFile":"Paediatrics_Nutrition_Accepted.md","_genhy":false},{"id":"EB-paediatrics-nutrition-accepted-Q89","spec":"eb-nutrition","topic":0,"tag":"Egyptian book","kind":"Other","q":"The dietary source of iodine","o":["Seafood","Iodisedsalt","Both of the above","None of the above"],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":2,"ex":"Seafood and iodized salt are recognized sources of dietary iodine. Iodized salt is a public-health strategy to prevent iodine-deficiency disorders.","more":"Seafood and iodized salt are recognized sources of dietary iodine. Iodized salt is a public-health strategy to prevent iodine-deficiency disorders.\n\nCurriculum alignment: Growth & Nutrition 2024, p.9 (micronutrients); Endocrine 2025, thyroid-related topics.\n\nHigh-yield point: Iodine deficiency can cause goiter and hypothyroidism; excess iodine may also alter thyroid function.","ao":true,"eb":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q89 — Dietary sources of iodine","aoSource":"MCQ د عرفة متقسم · PDF sequence page 28 · printed page 37 · answer-key page 34 · original question 89.","aoSystem":"التغذية","aoPart":"","kx":false,"ebSourceFile":"Paediatrics_Nutrition_Accepted.md","_genhy":false},{"id":"EB-paediatrics-nutrition-accepted-Q97","spec":"eb-nutrition","topic":0,"tag":"Egyptian book","kind":"Diagnosis","q":"The early sign of the rickets in6 month","o":["Craniotabes","Rickety Rosary","Double Malleoli","Knock Knee"],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":0,"ex":"Craniotabes (softening of the occipital/parietal skull bones) may be an early sign of infantile rickets. Rachitic rosary, malleolar widening and genu valgum generally occur later.","more":"Craniotabes (softening of the occipital/parietal skull bones) may be an early sign of infantile rickets. Rachitic rosary, malleolar widening and genu valgum generally occur later.\n\nCurriculum alignment: Growth & Nutrition 2024, pp.19–21 (rickets clinical features).\n\nHigh-yield point: Craniotabes can also be seen in newborns for other reasons; interpret in context and with biochemical/radiographic findings.","ao":true,"eb":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q97 — Early sign of rickets at six months","aoSource":"MCQ د عرفة متقسم · PDF sequence page 28 · printed page 37 · answer-key page 34 · original question 97.","aoSystem":"التغذية","aoPart":"","kx":false,"ebSourceFile":"Paediatrics_Nutrition_Accepted.md","_genhy":false},{"id":"EB-paediatrics-nutrition-accepted-Q99","spec":"eb-nutrition","topic":0,"tag":"Egyptian book","kind":"Diagnosis","q":"Kwashiorkor is characterized by all, except","o":["Dermatitis","Edema","Bag sign","Alertness"],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":3,"ex":"Kwashiorkor is associated with edema, dermatosis, hair changes (including flag sign), apathy and reduced activity/interest in surroundings. The child is not typically described as alert and well-interested.","more":"Kwashiorkor is associated with edema, dermatosis, hair changes (including flag sign), apathy and reduced activity/interest in surroundings. The child is not typically described as alert and well-interested.\n\nCurriculum alignment: Growth & Nutrition 2024, pp.12–14 (kwashiorkor clinical features).\n\nHigh-yield point: Edema may mask weight loss; look for skin, hair and behavioral changes.","ao":true,"eb":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q99 — Characteristic feature absent in kwashiorkor","aoSource":"MCQ د عرفة متقسم · PDF sequence page 28 · printed page 37 · answer-key page 34 · original question 99.","aoSystem":"التغذية","aoPart":"","kx":false,"ebSourceFile":"Paediatrics_Nutrition_Accepted.md","_genhy":false},{"id":"EB-paediatrics-nutrition-accepted-Q108","spec":"eb-nutrition","topic":0,"tag":"Egyptian book","kind":"Complications","q":"Mostlydeathinproteinenergymalnutrition(PEM) is due to all, except","o":["Hypothermia","Congestive cardiac failure(CCF)","Worm infestation","Electrolyte imbalance"],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":2,"ex":"Severe PEM mortality is commonly associated with hypoglycemia, hypothermia, severe infection/sepsis, dehydration, electrolyte imbalance and cardiac failure. Worm infestation can worsen malnutrition but is not usually listed among the principal immediate causes of death.","more":"Severe PEM mortality is commonly associated with hypoglycemia, hypothermia, severe infection/sepsis, dehydration, electrolyte imbalance and cardiac failure. Worm infestation can worsen malnutrition but is not usually listed among the principal immediate causes of death.\n\nCurriculum alignment: Growth & Nutrition 2024, pp.13–15 (PEM complications and stabilization treatment).\n\nHigh-yield point: Stabilization prioritizes hypoglycemia, hypothermia, dehydration, infection and electrolyte disturbance.","ao":true,"eb":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q108 — Common cause of death in PEM: exception","aoSource":"MCQ د عرفة متقسم · PDF sequence page 29 · printed page 38 · answer-key page 34 · original question 108.","aoSystem":"التغذية","aoPart":"","kx":false,"ebSourceFile":"Paediatrics_Nutrition_Accepted.md","_genhy":false},{"id":"EB-paediatrics-nutrition-accepted-Q110","spec":"eb-nutrition","topic":0,"tag":"Egyptian book","kind":"Diagnosis","q":"Flag sign is seen in","o":["Kwashiorkor","Marasmas","Pellagra","Hypothyroidism"],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":0,"ex":"The flag sign is alternating light and dark bands in hair due to periods of nutritional deprivation and partial recovery; it is classically associated with kwashiorkor.","more":"The flag sign is alternating light and dark bands in hair due to periods of nutritional deprivation and partial recovery; it is classically associated with kwashiorkor.\n\nCurriculum alignment: Growth & Nutrition 2024, p.12 (kwashiorkor hair changes and flag sign).\n\nHigh-yield point: Hair changes reflect the timing of protein-energy deprivation.","ao":true,"eb":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q110 — Flag sign in hair","aoSource":"MCQ د عرفة متقسم · PDF sequence page 29 · printed page 38 · answer-key page 34 · original question 110.","aoSystem":"التغذية","aoPart":"","kx":false,"ebSourceFile":"Paediatrics_Nutrition_Accepted.md","_genhy":false},{"id":"EB-paediatrics-nutrition-accepted-Q111","spec":"eb-nutrition","topic":0,"tag":"Egyptian book","kind":"Pathophysiology","q":"Serum cholesterol is high is all, expect","o":["Kwashiorkor","Diabetesmellitus","Primary biliary cirrhosis","Nephrotic syndrome"],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":0,"ex":"Serum cholesterol is often low in severe kwashiorkor because of impaired hepatic lipoprotein synthesis and altered fat transport. Diabetes, primary biliary cholangitis and nephrotic syndrome may be associated with elevated cholesterol.","more":"Serum cholesterol is often low in severe kwashiorkor because of impaired hepatic lipoprotein synthesis and altered fat transport. Diabetes, primary biliary cholangitis and nephrotic syndrome may be associated with elevated cholesterol.\n\nCurriculum alignment: Growth & Nutrition 2024, pp.12–14 (kwashiorkor and fatty liver); nephrology/hepatology curriculum where relevant.\n\nHigh-yield point: Kwashiorkor may cause fatty liver despite reduced circulating lipoprotein/cholesterol production.","ao":true,"eb":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q111 — Condition in which serum cholesterol is not high","aoSource":"MCQ د عرفة متقسم · PDF sequence page 29 · printed page 38 · answer-key page 34 · original question 111.","aoSystem":"التغذية","aoPart":"","kx":false,"ebSourceFile":"Paediatrics_Nutrition_Accepted.md","_genhy":false},{"id":"EB-paediatrics-nutrition-accepted-Q112","spec":"eb-nutrition","topic":0,"tag":"Egyptian book","kind":"Investigation","q":"The earliest indicator of protein energy malnutrition (PEM)is","o":["Hair changes","Edema","Underweight for age","Restlessness"],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":2,"ex":"Faltering weight gain/underweight is often an early measurable sign of inadequate energy intake. Hair changes and edema generally occur later; restlessness is nonspecific. The precise indicator depends on the growth chart and nutritional classification used.","more":"Faltering weight gain/underweight is often an early measurable sign of inadequate energy intake. Hair changes and edema generally occur later; restlessness is nonspecific. The precise indicator depends on the growth chart and nutritional classification used.\n\nCurriculum alignment: Growth & Nutrition 2024, pp.4, 10 and 16 (weight monitoring, malnutrition classification and FTT).\n\nHigh-yield point: A downward weight trajectory may precede overt clinical signs.","ao":true,"eb":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q112 — Earliest indicator of protein-energy malnutrition","aoSource":"MCQ د عرفة متقسم · PDF sequence page 30 · printed page 39 · answer-key page 34 · original question 112.","aoSystem":"التغذية","aoPart":"","kx":false,"ebSourceFile":"Paediatrics_Nutrition_Accepted.md","_genhy":false},{"id":"EB-paediatrics-nutrition-accepted-Q113","spec":"eb-nutrition","topic":0,"tag":"Egyptian book","kind":"Other","q":"The energy requirement of an infant is","o":["80-90 Kcal/kg","50-60 Kcal/kg","60-70 Kcal/kg","110-120 Kcal/kg"],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":3,"ex":"The course table gives approximately 115 kcal/kg/day for infants aged 0–6 months and 95 kcal/kg/day for 6–12 months. The selected 110–120 kcal/kg/day is an approximate infant requirement, particularly for younger infants.","more":"The course table gives approximately 115 kcal/kg/day for infants aged 0–6 months and 95 kcal/kg/day for 6–12 months. The selected 110–120 kcal/kg/day is an approximate infant requirement, particularly for younger infants.\n\nCurriculum alignment: Growth & Nutrition 2024, p.9 (age-specific energy requirements).\n\nHigh-yield point: Energy requirement per kilogram falls with age during childhood.","ao":true,"eb":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q113 — Energy requirement of an infant","aoSource":"MCQ د عرفة متقسم · PDF sequence page 30 · printed page 39 · answer-key page 34 · original question 113.","aoSystem":"التغذية","aoPart":"","kx":false,"ebSourceFile":"Paediatrics_Nutrition_Accepted.md","_genhy":false},{"id":"EB-paediatrics-nutrition-accepted-Q117","spec":"eb-nutrition","topic":0,"tag":"Egyptian book","kind":"Diagnosis","q":"Kwashiorkor is diagnosed in growth retarded children a long with","o":["Ede me and mental retardation","Hypopigmentation and anaemia","Edema and hypopigmentation","Hepatomegaly and anaemia"],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":2,"ex":"Kwashiorkor is characterized by edema and skin/hair changes, including hypopigmentation or depigmented areas; hepatomegaly from fatty liver can also occur. In the presented choices, edema with hypopigmentation is the best combination.","more":"Kwashiorkor is characterized by edema and skin/hair changes, including hypopigmentation or depigmented areas; hepatomegaly from fatty liver can also occur. In the presented choices, edema with hypopigmentation is the best combination.\n\nCurriculum alignment: Growth & Nutrition 2024, pp.12–14 (kwashiorkor, edema and skin changes).\n\nHigh-yield point: Nutritional edema distinguishes kwashiorkor from uncomplicated marasmus.","ao":true,"eb":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q117 — Kwashiorkor in a growth-retarded child","aoSource":"MCQ د عرفة متقسم · PDF sequence page 30 · printed page 39 · answer-key page 34 · original question 117.","aoSystem":"التغذية","aoPart":"","kx":false,"ebSourceFile":"Paediatrics_Nutrition_Accepted.md","_genhy":false},{"id":"EB-paediatrics-nutrition-accepted-Q118","spec":"eb-nutrition","topic":0,"tag":"Egyptian book","kind":"Pathophysiology","q":"A true about nutritional rickets","o":["Decreased production of ost e oid","Decreasedcorticalbone","Decreased calcification of growing bone","Decreased calcification of mature d bone"],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":2,"ex":"Nutritional rickets is defective mineralization of growing bone and growth-plate cartilage, caused by inadequate vitamin D and/or calcium availability.","more":"Nutritional rickets is defective mineralization of growing bone and growth-plate cartilage, caused by inadequate vitamin D and/or calcium availability.\n\nCurriculum alignment: Growth & Nutrition 2024, p.19 (definition of rickets); pp.20–21 (pathogenesis).\n\nHigh-yield point: Rickets affects the growth plate in children; osteomalacia affects mineralization of mature bone.","ao":true,"eb":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q118 — Pathology of nutritional rickets","aoSource":"MCQ د عرفة متقسم · PDF sequence page 30 · printed page 39 · answer-key page 34 · original question 118.","aoSystem":"التغذية","aoPart":"","kx":false,"ebSourceFile":"Paediatrics_Nutrition_Accepted.md","_genhy":false},{"id":"EB-paediatrics-nutrition-accepted-Q123","spec":"eb-nutrition","topic":0,"tag":"Egyptian book","kind":"Other","q":"Vitamin K deficiency occurs in the following, except","o":["Biliary obstruction","Oral antibiotic therapy","Artificial milk feeds","Malabsorption state"],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":2,"ex":"Vitamin K deficiency can occur with biliary obstruction/cholestasis, malabsorption and prolonged antibiotics that reduce intestinal vitamin K production. Artificial formula is generally fortified with vitamin K, making it less likely to cause deficiency than exclusive breastfeeding without prophylaxis.","more":"Vitamin K deficiency can occur with biliary obstruction/cholestasis, malabsorption and prolonged antibiotics that reduce intestinal vitamin K production. Artificial formula is generally fortified with vitamin K, making it less likely to cause deficiency than exclusive breastfeeding without prophylaxis.\n\nCurriculum alignment: Growth & Nutrition 2024, pp.30–32 (breast milk composition and vitamin K); Neonatology 2024–2025 (newborn vitamin K prophylaxis).\n\nHigh-yield point: All newborns should receive recommended vitamin K prophylaxis regardless of feeding method.","ao":true,"eb":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q123 — Vitamin K deficiency: exception","aoSource":"MCQ د عرفة متقسم · PDF sequence page 30 · printed page 39 · answer-key page 34 · original question 123.","aoSystem":"التغذية","aoPart":"","kx":false,"ebSourceFile":"Paediatrics_Nutrition_Accepted.md","_genhy":false},{"id":"EB-paediatrics-nutrition-accepted-Q128","spec":"eb-nutrition","topic":0,"tag":"Egyptian book","kind":"Physiology","q":"Human colostrum contains more of the following nutrients than mature milk except","o":["Lactose","Minerals","Protein","Vitamin A"],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":0,"ex":"Colostrum is relatively rich in protein, immunoglobulins, minerals and vitamin A, while mature milk has a higher lactose concentration. Thus lactose is the exception.","more":"Colostrum is relatively rich in protein, immunoglobulins, minerals and vitamin A, while mature milk has a higher lactose concentration. Thus lactose is the exception.\n\nCurriculum alignment: Growth & Nutrition 2024, pp.27–32 (colostrum, breast milk composition and advantages).\n\nHigh-yield point: Colostrum is concentrated, immune-rich early milk; small volumes are physiologically appropriate.","ao":true,"eb":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q128 — Colostrum: nutrient present in lower concentration than mature milk","aoSource":"MCQ د عرفة متقسم · PDF sequence page 31 · printed page 40 · answer-key page 34 · original question 128.","aoSystem":"التغذية","aoPart":"","kx":false,"ebSourceFile":"Paediatrics_Nutrition_Accepted.md","_genhy":false},{"id":"EB-paediatrics-nutrition-accepted-Q130","spec":"eb-nutrition","topic":0,"tag":"Egyptian book","kind":"Treatment","q":"This is not used in ORS","o":["Na+","Ka+","Glucose with salt","Glucose without salt"],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":3,"ex":"Oral rehydration solution requires glucose and electrolytes, especially sodium and potassium, in an appropriate balance. Glucose without salt is not ORS and does not provide sodium replacement.","more":"Oral rehydration solution requires glucose and electrolytes, especially sodium and potassium, in an appropriate balance. Glucose without salt is not ORS and does not provide sodium replacement.\n\nCurriculum alignment: Growth & Nutrition 2024, p.15 (rehydration solution in severe malnutrition); broader dehydration/ORS content in pediatric curriculum.\n\nHigh-yield point: Glucose facilitates sodium and water absorption through intestinal sodium-glucose cotransport.","ao":true,"eb":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q130 — Component combination not used in ORS","aoSource":"MCQ د عرفة متقسم · PDF sequence page 31 · printed page 40 · answer-key page 34 · original question 130.","aoSystem":"التغذية","aoPart":"","kx":false,"ebSourceFile":"Paediatrics_Nutrition_Accepted.md","_genhy":false},{"id":"EB-paediatrics-nutrition-accepted-Q131","spec":"eb-nutrition","topic":0,"tag":"Egyptian book","kind":"Pathophysiology","q":"Kwashiorkor is due to","o":["Protein deficiency","Protein and energy deficiency","Energy deficiency","Malabsorption of vitamin"],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":1,"ex":"Kwashiorkor is a form of severe acute malnutrition with nutritional edema and an inadequate diet; older teaching emphasizes protein deficiency relative to energy, while current understanding is multifactorial and includes infection, gut dysfunction, oxidative stress and metabolic adaptation. The source key chooses protein and energy deficiency.","more":"Kwashiorkor is a form of severe acute malnutrition with nutritional edema and an inadequate diet; older teaching emphasizes protein deficiency relative to energy, while current understanding is multifactorial and includes infection, gut dysfunction, oxidative stress and metabolic adaptation. The source key chooses protein and energy deficiency.\n\nCurriculum alignment: Growth & Nutrition 2024, pp.10–13 (PEM classification and kwashiorkor).\n\nHigh-yield point: Avoid an overly simplistic “protein-only deficiency” model; severe acute malnutrition is multifactorial.","ao":true,"eb":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q131 — Etiology of kwashiorkor","aoSource":"MCQ د عرفة متقسم · PDF sequence page 31 · printed page 40 · answer-key page 34 · original question 131.","aoSystem":"التغذية","aoPart":"","kx":false,"ebSourceFile":"Paediatrics_Nutrition_Accepted.md","_genhy":false},{"id":"EB-paediatrics-nutrition-accepted-Q137","spec":"eb-nutrition","topic":0,"tag":"Egyptian book","kind":"Investigation","q":"The following are the radiographic features of rickets, except","o":["Increasedwidthofthegrowthplate","Decreased bone density","Ricketyrosary","Subperiosteal bleeding"],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":3,"ex":"Rickets radiographs show widened growth plates, metaphyseal cupping/fraying, reduced bone density and rachitic rosary. Subperiosteal hemorrhage is more characteristic of scurvy.","more":"Rickets radiographs show widened growth plates, metaphyseal cupping/fraying, reduced bone density and rachitic rosary. Subperiosteal hemorrhage is more characteristic of scurvy.\n\nCurriculum alignment: Growth & Nutrition 2024, pp.19–21 (rickets radiographic and clinical features).\n\nHigh-yield point: Rickets affects metaphyses of rapidly growing bones.","ao":true,"eb":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q137 — Radiographic feature not seen in rickets","aoSource":"MCQ د عرفة متقسم · PDF sequence page 31 · printed page 40 · answer-key page 34 · original question 137.","aoSystem":"التغذية","aoPart":"","kx":false,"ebSourceFile":"Paediatrics_Nutrition_Accepted.md","_genhy":false},{"id":"EB-paediatrics-nutrition-accepted-Q138","spec":"eb-nutrition","topic":0,"tag":"Egyptian book","kind":"Prognosis","q":"Poor prognostic factors in a child with kwashiorkor include all, except","o":["Hypothermia","Pupura","Hepatomegaly","Dermotosis"],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":2,"ex":"Poor prognostic features in severe malnutrition include hypothermia, purpura, severe dermatosis, infection, poor appetite and complications. Hepatomegaly from fatty liver is common in kwashiorkor but is not generally listed as the poor prognostic feature among these options.","more":"Poor prognostic features in severe malnutrition include hypothermia, purpura, severe dermatosis, infection, poor appetite and complications. Hepatomegaly from fatty liver is common in kwashiorkor but is not generally listed as the poor prognostic feature among these options.\n\nCurriculum alignment: Growth & Nutrition 2024, pp.13–15 (PEM complications, labs, stabilization).\n\nHigh-yield point: Assess danger signs and complications; do not infer prognosis from hepatomegaly alone.","ao":true,"eb":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q138 — Poor prognostic factors in kwashiorkor: exception","aoSource":"MCQ د عرفة متقسم · PDF sequence page 31 · printed page 40 · answer-key page 34 · original question 138.","aoSystem":"التغذية","aoPart":"","kx":false,"ebSourceFile":"Paediatrics_Nutrition_Accepted.md","_genhy":false},{"id":"EB-paediatrics-nutrition-accepted-Q140","spec":"eb-nutrition","topic":0,"tag":"Egyptian book","kind":"Diagnosis","q":"Lactase deficiency in children is characterized by","o":["Dumpingsyndrome","Intraventricularhaemorrhage","Abdominal bloating","Meningitis"],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":2,"ex":"Lactase deficiency causes lactose malabsorption, leading to osmotic diarrhea, gas, abdominal distension/bloating and sometimes acidic stools.","more":"Lactase deficiency causes lactose malabsorption, leading to osmotic diarrhea, gas, abdominal distension/bloating and sometimes acidic stools.\n\nCurriculum alignment: Growth & Nutrition 2024, nutritional and feeding sections; GIT 2024–2025/2025, carbohydrate malabsorption concepts.\n\nHigh-yield point: Symptoms occur after lactose ingestion and are dose-dependent; primary lactase non-persistence is uncommon in young infants.","ao":true,"eb":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q140 — Clinical feature of lactose deficiency","aoSource":"MCQ د عرفة متقسم · PDF sequence page 32 · printed page 41 · answer-key page 34 · original question 140.","aoSystem":"التغذية","aoPart":"","kx":false,"ebSourceFile":"Paediatrics_Nutrition_Accepted.md","_genhy":false},{"id":"EB-paediatrics-nutrition-accepted-Q143","spec":"eb-nutrition","topic":0,"tag":"Egyptian book","kind":"Physiology","q":"The function of glucose in oral rehydration solution (ORS)","o":["Increases sodium absorption","Increasesosmolalityof ORS","Givessweettasteto ORS","Increases Na+and K+pumpactivity"],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":0,"ex":"Glucose is co-transported with sodium across the intestinal brush border via SGLT1; water follows sodium and glucose absorption. This remains effective in many secretory diarrheal illnesses.","more":"Glucose is co-transported with sodium across the intestinal brush border via SGLT1; water follows sodium and glucose absorption. This remains effective in many secretory diarrheal illnesses.\n\nCurriculum alignment: Growth & Nutrition 2024, p.15 (rehydration solution); pediatric GIT/diarrhea course topics.\n\nHigh-yield point: ORS works because sodium-glucose cotransport remains functional in most diarrheal diseases.","ao":true,"eb":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q143 — Function of glucose in ORS","aoSource":"MCQ د عرفة متقسم · PDF sequence page 32 · printed page 41 · answer-key page 35 · original question 143.","aoSystem":"التغذية","aoPart":"","kx":false,"ebSourceFile":"Paediatrics_Nutrition_Accepted.md","_genhy":false},{"id":"EB-paediatrics-nutrition-accepted-Q147","spec":"eb-nutrition","topic":0,"tag":"Egyptian book","kind":"Physiology","q":"Human colostrum has a relatively higher concentration of all, except","o":["Protein","Ig A","Fat","Sodium"],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":2,"ex":"Compared with mature milk, colostrum is relatively richer in protein, secretory IgA and sodium; mature milk generally has a higher fat concentration as milk volume increases.","more":"Compared with mature milk, colostrum is relatively richer in protein, secretory IgA and sodium; mature milk generally has a higher fat concentration as milk volume increases.\n\nCurriculum alignment: Growth & Nutrition 2024, pp.27–32 (colostrum and milk composition).\n\nHigh-yield point: Colostrum provides immune protection and concentrated nutrients in small volumes.","ao":true,"eb":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q147 — Colostrum concentration: exception","aoSource":"MCQ د عرفة متقسم · PDF sequence page 32 · printed page 41 · answer-key page 34 · original question 147.","aoSystem":"التغذية","aoPart":"","kx":false,"ebSourceFile":"Paediatrics_Nutrition_Accepted.md","_genhy":false},{"id":"EB-paediatrics-nutrition-accepted-Q149","spec":"eb-nutrition","topic":0,"tag":"Egyptian book","kind":"Diagnosis","q":"Deficient mineralization in epiphyseal growth cartilage is seen in","o":["Rickets","Scurvy","Osteomalacia","Hyperparathyroidism"],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":0,"ex":"Defective mineralization of growing epiphyseal cartilage is the defining pathology of rickets. Scurvy is defective collagen synthesis; osteomalacia is defective mineralization of mature bone.","more":"Defective mineralization of growing epiphyseal cartilage is the defining pathology of rickets. Scurvy is defective collagen synthesis; osteomalacia is defective mineralization of mature bone.\n\nCurriculum alignment: Growth & Nutrition 2024, p.19 (rickets definition) and pp.20–21 (pathogenesis).\n\nHigh-yield point: In a growing child, defective growth-plate mineralization produces rickets; in adults it produces osteomalacia.","ao":true,"eb":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q149 — Defective mineralization at epiphyseal growth cartilage","aoSource":"MCQ د عرفة متقسم · PDF sequence page 32 · printed page 41 · answer-key page 35 · original question 149.","aoSystem":"التغذية","aoPart":"","kx":false,"ebSourceFile":"Paediatrics_Nutrition_Accepted.md","_genhy":false},{"id":"EB-paediatrics-nutrition-accepted-Q150","spec":"eb-nutrition","topic":0,"tag":"Egyptian book","kind":"Diagnosis","q":"All are seen in kwashiorkor, except","o":["Depigmented areas in skin","Fatty infiltration of the liver","Oedema","Fatty infiltration of pancreas"],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":3,"ex":"Kwashiorkor is associated with edema, flaky-paint dermatosis, hair changes and fatty liver. Fatty infiltration of the pancreas is not a typical defining feature.","more":"Kwashiorkor is associated with edema, flaky-paint dermatosis, hair changes and fatty liver. Fatty infiltration of the pancreas is not a typical defining feature.\n\nCurriculum alignment: Growth & Nutrition 2024, pp.12–14 (kwashiorkor clinical features and complications).\n\nHigh-yield point: The classic triad includes edema, dermatosis and hair changes; hepatic steatosis is common.","ao":true,"eb":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q150 — Finding not seen in kwashiorkor","aoSource":"MCQ د عرفة متقسم · PDF sequence page 33 · printed page 42 · answer-key page 34 · original question 150.","aoSystem":"التغذية","aoPart":"","kx":false,"ebSourceFile":"Paediatrics_Nutrition_Accepted.md","_genhy":false},{"id":"EB-paediatrics-nutrition-accepted-Q16","spec":"eb-nutrition","topic":0,"tag":"Egyptian book","kind":"Pathophysiology","q":"The fat which disappears last in PEM is","o":["Buttockfat","Fatintheback","Buccal pad of fat","Fat in the abdomen"],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":2,"ex":"In severe wasting, subcutaneous fat is lost in a characteristic pattern; facial fat (including the buccal fat pads) is relatively preserved until later, producing the “old-man” appearance as wasting progresses. The question’s sequence is a traditional descriptive teaching point.","more":"In severe wasting, subcutaneous fat is lost in a characteristic pattern; facial fat (including the buccal fat pads) is relatively preserved until later, producing the “old-man” appearance as wasting progresses. The question’s sequence is a traditional descriptive teaching point.\n\nCurriculum alignment: Growth & Nutrition 2024, pp.12–13 (marasmus: loss of subcutaneous fat in abdomen, buttocks/thighs and face).\n\nHigh-yield point: Marasmus causes marked loss of subcutaneous fat; the face becomes affected later.","ao":true,"eb":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q16 — Fat lost last in protein-energy malnutrition","aoSource":"MCQ د عرفة متقسم · PDF sequence page 20 · printed page 29 · answer-key page 34 · original question 16.","aoSystem":"التغذية","aoPart":"","kx":false,"ebSourceFile":"Paediatrics_Nutrition_Accepted.md","_genhy":false},{"id":"EB-paediatrics-nutrition-accepted-Q17","spec":"eb-nutrition","topic":0,"tag":"Egyptian book","kind":"Pathophysiology","q":"The fat re appears first on recovery is","o":["Buccal pad of fat","Fat in the abdomen","Fatinthebuttock","Fatinback"],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":0,"ex":"During nutritional recovery, fat deposition may first become noticeable in the face/buccal pads, followed by other depots. This is a traditional clinical observation rather than a precise validated monitoring endpoint.","more":"During nutritional recovery, fat deposition may first become noticeable in the face/buccal pads, followed by other depots. This is a traditional clinical observation rather than a precise validated monitoring endpoint.\n\nCurriculum alignment: Growth & Nutrition 2024, pp.12–13 (distribution of fat loss in marasmus; facial fat).\n\nHigh-yield point: Monitor recovery using weight gain, appetite, edema resolution, clinical state and serial anthropometry rather than fat distribution alone.","ao":true,"eb":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q17 — Fat reappearing first during recovery","aoSource":"MCQ د عرفة متقسم · PDF sequence page 20 · printed page 29 · answer-key page 34 · original question 17.","aoSystem":"التغذية","aoPart":"","kx":false,"ebSourceFile":"Paediatrics_Nutrition_Accepted.md","_genhy":false},{"id":"EB-paediatrics-nutrition-accepted-Q54","spec":"eb-nutrition","topic":0,"tag":"Egyptian book","kind":"Pathophysiology","q":"The following organs are involved in Niacin deficiency, except","o":["Skin","GIT","CNS","CVS"],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":3,"ex":"Niacin deficiency causes pellagra, classically dermatitis, diarrhea and dementia (the three Ds), reflecting skin, gastrointestinal and neurologic involvement. Cardiovascular disease is not one of the classic defining organ-system features.","more":"Niacin deficiency causes pellagra, classically dermatitis, diarrhea and dementia (the three Ds), reflecting skin, gastrointestinal and neurologic involvement. Cardiovascular disease is not one of the classic defining organ-system features.\n\nCurriculum alignment: Growth & Nutrition 2024, p.14 (vitamin deficiency manifestations).\n\nHigh-yield point: Remember the three Ds of pellagra: dermatitis, diarrhea, dementia.","ao":true,"eb":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q54 — Organ systems affected in niacin deficiency: exception","aoSource":"MCQ د عرفة متقسم · PDF sequence page 23 · printed page 32 · answer-key page 34 · original question 54.","aoSystem":"التغذية","aoPart":"","kx":false,"ebSourceFile":"Paediatrics_Nutrition_Accepted.md","_genhy":false},{"id":"EB-paediatrics-nutrition-accepted-Q86","spec":"eb-nutrition","topic":0,"tag":"Egyptian book","kind":"Physiology","q":"The vitamin synthesized in human body include, except","o":["Vitamin K","B complex","Vitamin A","Vitamin D"],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":2,"ex":"Humans synthesize vitamin D3 in the skin with UVB exposure; intestinal microbiota synthesize some vitamin K and certain B vitamins. Vitamin A cannot be synthesized de novo and must come from diet (preformed retinoids or provitamin A carotenoids).","more":"Humans synthesize vitamin D3 in the skin with UVB exposure; intestinal microbiota synthesize some vitamin K and certain B vitamins. Vitamin A cannot be synthesized de novo and must come from diet (preformed retinoids or provitamin A carotenoids).\n\nCurriculum alignment: Growth & Nutrition 2024, pp.9 and 19 (vitamins and vitamin D synthesis).\n\nHigh-yield point: Vitamin D production depends on UVB exposure; vitamin A must be obtained from the diet.","ao":true,"eb":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q86 — Vitamin synthesized in the human body: exception","aoSource":"MCQ د عرفة متقسم · PDF sequence page 27 · printed page 36 · answer-key page 34 · original question 86.","aoSystem":"التغذية","aoPart":"","kx":false,"ebSourceFile":"Paediatrics_Nutrition_Accepted.md","_genhy":false}]);
